          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Given an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            on some
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            %em stabilizer group
            of that point (that subspace) is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
            whose action leaves the point (the subspace) fixed,
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant"} invariant
          %p
            The importance of stabilizer subgroups for the general development of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            was famously highlighted in (
            %a{:href => "#Klein1872"}> Klein 1872
            ) in the context of what has come to be known the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Erlangen+program"} Erlangen program
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein+geometry"} Klein geometry
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
          %p
            Sometimes (such as in the context of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wigner+classification"} Wigner classification
            = succeed "." do
              %em little groups
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#TraditionalDefinition Traditional
          %p
            Given an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\times X\to X
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            on a set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            %strong stabilizer subgroup
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            (also called the
            %strong isotropy group
            of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            that leave
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            fixed:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Stab
                    %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Stab_G(x) = \{g \in G \mid g\circ x = x\}
                  \\,.
          %p
            If all stabilizer groups are trivial, then the action is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em free action
          %h3#GeneralAbstract Homotopy-theoretic formulation
          %p
            We reformulate the traditional definition
            %a{:href => "#TraditionalDefinition"} above
            from the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho\colon G \times X \to X
            is equivalently encoded in its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+sequence"} fiber sequence
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grpd"} Grpd
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \to X//G \to \mathbf{B}G
                  \\,,
          %p
            where the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X//G
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
            itself,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is regarded as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-truncated"} 0-truncated
            groupoid.
          %p
            This fiber sequence may be thought of as being the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+bundle"} associated bundle
            to the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
